Dear Peter,

Apologies for the delayed outreach. I have no power, water, or internet, and it took me several days to leave my house. Here’s an update on the ongoing winter storm and its impact on our community in Davidson County.

First and foremost, restoring power remains the top priority. Prolonged extreme cold has placed significant strain on our electric and water systems, and outages are continuing in parts of Nashville. Utility crews are working around the clock, and the State has taken steps to expedite restoration by authorizing TDOT to clear non-state roads so line workers can access damaged areas more quickly.

Current conditions in Davidson County

Extremely cold temperatures and icy, refreezing road conditions continue.
Power outages are ongoing in some neighborhoods.
Some water systems are experiencing impacts; please follow guidance from your local water provider.
Cellular service may be limited in areas due to power loss, but 911 remains available for life-threatening emergencies.

Welfare checks

Davidson County has its own process for welfare checks.If you are concerned about someone’s safety, please submit a request through Metro Nashville’s official online hub. Do not use the statewide hotline for Davidson County residents.

Warming centers

Warming centers remain open across Nashville, with some transitioning into overnight shelters. Nashville residents needing transportation to a warming station can call 615-401-1712. Callers should share their location, needs and whether pets are included.Hours and availability vary, so please check Metro Nashville’s official shelter information or contact the local Emergency Management Agency for assistance.

Safety reminders

Avoid driving if at all possible. Ice and refreezing conditions make roads extremely hazardous.
Stay clear of downed power lines and assume all lines are energized.
Limit time outdoors to avoid hypothermia and frostbite.
Use extreme caution when heating your home:

Never use generators, grills, or propane heaters indoors.Keep generators at least 20 feet from your home and never connect them directly to your electrical system.
Use space heaters safely and never leave them unattended.
If you lose power, discard perishable food that has been above 40°F for more than two hours.

If you need help

If you or someone you know needs assistance with storm-related cleanup, Crisis Cleanup is available at 844-965-1386. Services are free, but availability may be limited.
Check on elderly neighbors, people with disabilities, and anyone relying on medical equipment.

The State remains under a declared State of Emergency, and I am in regular communication with local and state officials as response and recovery continue. I will continue to share updates as conditions change.

I spent yesterday on the phone with the National Guard, TEMA, TDOT, and the Office of Emergency Management specifically to push for additional resources in our hardest-hit areas. As a result of those conversations, 50 National Guard troops and two skid steers are being deployed to South and West Nashville to assist with debris removal, which is a critical step in clearing access so NES lineworkers can restore power more quickly and safely.
I share your concern about the pace of restoration and the strain on NES crews. While only trained lineworkers can perform electrical repairs, clearing trees and debris is often the bottleneck, and this additional state support will make an immediate difference there. I am also pressing for continued coordination between the city, state agencies, and NES to ensure resources are focused where outages have persisted the longest.
I know this does not make the current situation any easier, but please know that I am actively advocating for you and your neighbors and will continue pushing until power is restored and conditions stabilize.

Please stay safe, conserve energy where possible, and rely on official local sources for accurate information. If my office can help connect you to resources, don’t hesitate to reach out. My cell is 615.438.6338.

Stay safe out there,
